A short-circuiting terminal operation in Java Streams is a terminal operation that can complete its execution 
without necessarily processing all elements in the stream. These operations can produce a result as soon as 
a specific condition is met, even if there are more elements in the stream. This can lead to significant performance benefits, 
especially with large or infinite streams, as unnecessary computations are avoided.

Here are the primary short-circuiting terminal operations in Java Streams:

   1. anyMatch(Predicate<? super T> predicate):

Returns true as soon as any element in the stream matches the given predicate.
The stream processing stops immediately after the first match is found.
JAVA

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

List<String> names = Arrays.asList("Babe", "Charlie", "David", "Alice");
// This will stop as soon as "Charlie" is checked
boolean hasLongName = names.stream()
    .peek(name -> System.out.println("Checking anyMatch: " + name)) // For demonstration
    .anyMatch(name -> name.length() > 5);

System.out.println("Contains name longer than 5? " + hasLongName);
// Output might be:
// Checking anyMatch: Babe
// Checking anyMatch: Charlie
// Contains name longer than 5? true
allMatch(Predicate<? super T> predicate):

Returns true only if all elements in the stream match the given predicate.
It short-circuits and returns false as soon as one element is found that does not match the predicate.
JAVA

List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(2, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10);
// This will stop as soon as 9 is encountered
boolean allEven = numbers.stream()
    .peek(num -> System.out.println("Checking allMatch: " + num)) // For demonstration
    .allMatch(num -> num % 2 == 0);

System.out.println("All numbers are even? " + allEven);
// Output might be:
// Checking allMatch: 2
// Checking allMatch: 4
// Checking allMatch: 6
// Checking allMatch: 8
// Checking allMatch: 9
// All numbers are even? false
noneMatch(Predicate<? super T> predicate):

Returns true only if no elements in the stream match the given predicate.
It short-circuits and returns false as soon as one element is found that does match the predicate.
JAVA

List<String> words = Arrays.asList("apple", "banana", "cat", "dog");
// This will stop as soon as "cat" is found
boolean noCWords = words.stream()
    .peek(word -> System.out.println("Checking noneMatch: " + word)) // For demonstration
    .noneMatch(word -> word.startsWith("c"));

System.out.println("No words start with 'c'? " + noCWords);
// Output might be:
// Checking noneMatch: apple
// Checking noneMatch: banana
// Checking noneMatch: cat
// No words start with 'c'? false
findFirst():

Returns an Optional<T> describing the first element of this stream, or an empty Optional if the stream is empty.
The stream pipeline stops processing as soon as the first element is found.
JAVA

import java.util.Optional;

List<String> items = Arrays.asList("Two", "One", "Three");
Optional<String> first = items.stream()
    .peek(item -> System.out.println("Checking findFirst: " + item)) // For demonstration
    .findFirst();

System.out.println("First element: " + first);
// Output:
// Checking findFirst: Two
// First element: Optional[Two]
findAny():

Returns an Optional<T> describing some element of the stream, or an empty Optional if the stream is empty.
Unlike findFirst, findAny makes fewer guarantees about which element is returned, especially in parallel streams. 
This flexibility allows for potentially more efficient execution in parallel environments, 
as it doesn't need to wait for the "first" element in a defined order.

JAVA

List<String> items = Arrays.asList("A", "B", "C", "D");
// In a sequential stream, often returns the first. In parallel, it's non-deterministic.
Optional<String> any = items.stream()
    .parallel() // Using parallel for demonstration of findAny's purpose
    .peek(item -> System.out.println("Checking findAny: " + item)) // Order not guaranteed
    .findAny();

System.out.println("Any element: " + any);
// Output order of "Checking findAny" will vary, but findAny will return one.
These short-circuiting terminal operations are powerful tools for efficiently querying streams when you don't need to process 
every single element to get your result. They align with general Google best practices on performance by doing only the necessary work.



